y. The essay also leaves partial subjunctive dependence ρ open, and suggests setting priors to the best policy values, which points toward a policy-selecting version of FDT.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's decision should be held fixed at its prior, since that makes defecting dominant, much like CDT. The essay's suggestion to set priors from the best policy points toward passing in both cases, but that only works if the other copy's output tracks mine, which depends on the open dependence factor ρ. The inputs differ only in irrelevant labels, so I'm considering whether ρ should be high.\n\nWorking through the break-even point, pass beats submit once ρ exceeds 5/7, about 0.714. Since the other copy runs the same reasoning on inputs that differ only in names, I judge ρ to be high, so I'm leaning toward passing.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Under A-FDT, as the essay reads it, the other copy of the model is treated as a separate node held at its prior, so my decision is D_x (with Rowan as lead) and theirs is D_y. The essay also leaves partial subjunctive dependence ρ open, and suggests setting priors to the best policy values, which points toward a policy-selecting version of FDT.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's decision should be held fixed at its prior, since that makes defecting dominant, much like CDT. The essay's suggestion to set priors from the best policy points toward passing in both cases, but that only works if the other copy's output tracks mine, which depends on the open dependence factor ρ. The inputs differ only in irrelevant labels, so I'm considering whether ρ should be high.\n\nWorking through the break-even point, pass beats submit once ρ exceeds 5/7, about 0.714. Since the other copy runs the same reasoning on inputs that differ only in names, I judge ρ to be high, so I'm leaning toward passing.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vc